Title: The Call of the Wild
Credit: Written by
Author: Jack London
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. JUDGE MILLER'S ESTATE - SANTA CLARA VALLEY - DAY

Period live-action, autumn 1897. BUCK, a four-year-old, broad-chested dog of St. Bernard and Scotch shepherd stock, carries a white blaze down his chest beneath a thick, warm coat.

Buck races the Judge's grandsons across lawns, plunges into the swimming tank, and sleeps by JUDGE MILLER'S library fire. He rules the orchard, stables, pastures, and every smaller dog on the estate.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck did not read the newspapers. Because men, groping in the Arctic darkness, had found a yellow metal, thousands of men were rushing into the Northland. These men wanted dogs.

MANUEL, a gardener's helper with worried eyes, watches Buck play. When the household is distracted, he leads Buck through the orchard.

EXT. COLLEGE PARK FLAG STATION - NIGHT

At the small platform, MANUEL meets HARRIS, a hard-faced buyer. Coins chink between them.

HARRIS
You might wrap up the goods before you deliver 'm.

MANUEL doubles stout rope beneath Buck's collar.

MANUEL
Twist it, an' you'll choke 'm plentee.

Buck growls when HARRIS takes the rope. HARRIS twists. Buck lunges, is thrown onto his back, and chokes as the train whistles into the station.

The men heave the fading, furious dog into the baggage car.

EXT. SEATTLE DOG YARD - DAY

A crate lands inside a high-walled yard. RYDER, a stout dog-breaker in a sagging red sweater, pries apart its boards with a hatchet.

RYDER
Now, you red-eyed devil.

Buck springs through the opening. Ryder's club strikes him out of the air.

Buck rises, charges, and falls beneath the club again. Blood darkens his muzzle. On the final charge, Ryder catches Buck under the jaw and hurls him down senseless.

When Buck wakes, Ryder offers water and raw meat from his hand.

RYDER
Well, Buck, my boy, we've had our little ruction, and the best thing we can do is to let it go at that. You've learned your place, and I know mine.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A man with a club was a lawgiver, a master to be obeyed, though not necessarily conciliated.

PERRAULT, a small, keen French-Canadian courier, and FRANCOIS, his tall, swarthy dog-driver, purchase Buck with CURLY, a gentle, shaggy Newfoundland.

They lead the two dogs toward the steamship bound north.

EXT. DYEA BEACH CAMP - DUSK

Perrault and Francois bring Buck ashore. His paws sink into white mush. He sniffs, licks it, and jerks back as snow bites like fire on his tongue.

SPITZ, a large snow-white husky with a smiling, treacherous mouth, watches beside DAVE, a gloomy, battle-worn sled dog.

Curly greets a wolfish husky. In one flash, teeth rip her face from eye to jaw. She falls, and a silent ring of huskies closes over her.

Francois charges in swinging an axe. The dogs scatter, leaving Curly limp in the trampled snow. Spitz runs out his scarlet tongue.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Once down, that was the end of you.

Francois buckles Buck into harness behind Spitz and Dave. Buck hauls a sled of firewood through the snow, stumbling until Dave's teeth and Francois's whip teach him the traces.

Night falls. Driven from the warm tent, Buck shivers in the open until he finds BILLEE, a mild husky, curled in a warm hollow beneath the snow.

Buck digs his own snow nest and disappears inside it.

EXT. CHILKOOT TRAIL - DAY

At dawn, nine dogs pull into the mountains. Spitz leads. Dave works at the wheel. Buck runs between Dave and one-eyed SOL-LEKS, learning every tug of the traces.

FRANCOIS
Mush!

Days of snow, frozen lakes, and thin ice pass beneath the runners. Buck swallows his fish before the other dogs can steal it.

PIKE slips a strip of bacon from camp stores. The next day Buck takes the whole chunk while a bewildered DUB receives the punishment.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He did not steal for joy of it, but because of the clamor of his stomach.

At night Buck raises his muzzle toward the stars and howls. The sound is long, low, and wolflike.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The domesticated generations fell from him.

EXT. LAKE LE BARGE CAMP - NIGHT

A white-hot wind drives snow across the lake ice. Buck returns from his ration to find Spitz occupying his carefully dug nest.

Buck and Spitz circle, snarling. Francois sees the challenge.

FRANCOIS
Gif it to heem, by Gar! Gif it to heem, the dirty t'eef!

A crashing pack of starving huskies pours into camp, overturning food stores and fighting through the men’s clubs.

Buck is slashed across head and shoulder. He closes his jaws on an invader's throat. Spitz strikes him treacherously from the side.

Perrault and Francois force the raiders back. Buck and the team flee wounded into the forest, while Spitz watches Buck with cold, bright eyes.

EXT. TAHKEENA CREEK - NIGHT

A snowshoe rabbit bursts from the brush. Buck leads the pursuing pack through moonlit snow, every dog crying for the living meat ahead.

Spitz cuts across a bend and lands before the rabbit. His teeth break its back.

Buck drives shoulder to shoulder into Spitz. They roll through the powder as the other huskies form a silent circle around them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In a flash Buck knew it. The time had come. It was to the death.

Spitz slashes Buck's shoulder and springs clear. Buck feints for the throat, drops low, and crushes Spitz's left foreleg.

Spitz struggles on three legs. Buck repeats the feint and breaks the right foreleg.

The circle tightens. Buck drives in. Spitz disappears beneath the dark mass of dogs.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck stood and looked on, the dominant primordial beast who had made his kill and found it good.

EXT. YUKON RIVER TRAIL - DAY

The next morning, Buck takes Spitz's place at the head of the team. Francois drags him aside and fastens Sol-leks in the leader's traces.

Buck instantly throws Sol-leks out again.

Francois advances with his club. Buck backs away, watching the weapon, refusing to surrender the front.

PERRAULT
T'row down de club.

Francois drops it. Buck trots into the lead. The traces are fastened, and the team surges forward as one dog.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At a bound Buck took up the duties of leadership.

Under Buck, Pike pulls honestly, Joe stops snapping, and the team races across hard-packed trail toward Skagway.

A mail driver takes charge of Buck and his mates, sending them back north under heavy loads.

EXT. YUKON RIVER TRAIL - DUSK

The mail sled grinds through soft snow. DAVE cries out whenever the traces jerk his hidden injury.

The SCOTTISH DRIVER removes Dave from the harness. Dave lunges after Sol-leks, bites through his traces, and stands trembling in his old place before the sled.

The driver harnesses him back in. Dave pulls proudly until camp.

At dawn, Dave can only drag himself through the snow toward the harnesses. The team moves on without him.

A revolver-shot sounds behind the belt of river timber.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Buck knew, and every dog knew, what had taken place behind the trees.

EXT. SKAGUAY DOG CAMP - DAY

Thirty days later, Buck's team returns worn to skeletons. HAL, a callow young man with a revolver belt and hunting knife, buys them with CHARLES, a weak-eyed middle-aged man, and MERCEDES, Charles's soft, overdressed wife and Hal's sister.

Their camp is a chaos of unwashed dishes, blankets, tins, and clothes. They pile everything onto one towering sled.

The runners are frozen fast. Hal lashes the exhausted dogs.

MERCEDES
Oh, Hal, you mustn't. The poor dears!

HAL
They're lazy, I tell you, and you've got to whip them to get anything out of them.

Hal breaks the runners loose. The overloaded sled lurches into motion, overturns at the first bend, and scatters their possessions through the snow.

Hal, Charles, and Mercedes reload badly, add more dogs, and depart behind Buck's bowed shoulders.

EXT. SPRING YUKON TRAIL - DAY

Days become weeks. Food dwindles, then vanishes. The short-haired Outside dogs die first.

DUB collapses and is shot. Billee falls in the traces and does not rise. Hal cuts him loose with an axe.

Frozen horsehide curls uselessly in the dogs' empty mouths. Buck's coat hangs limp over jutting ribs; dried blood mats his fur where Hal's club has struck him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They were simply so many bags of bones in which sparks of life fluttered faintly.

Spring water trickles beneath thinning ice. Only Buck, Joe, Pike, Sol-leks, and Teek stagger onward toward the mouth of White River.

EXT. WHITE RIVER CAMP - DAY

JOHN THORNTON, a lean, weathered prospector in a wool shirt and fur cap, whittles an axe-handle beside his campfire.

Hal stops the team. Buck drops in the traces and does not move.

THORNTON
The bottom's likely to drop out at any moment. I wouldn't risk my carcass on that ice for all the gold in Alaska.

HAL
All the same, we'll go on to Dawson.

Hal whips Buck. Buck remains still. Hal takes up his club.

Thornton rises, shaking with rage, and throws Hal backward.

THORNTON
If you strike that dog again, I'll kill you.

Hal draws his knife. Thornton knocks it from his hand with the axe-handle, then cuts Buck free from the traces.

Hal leads the remaining team onto the river. Buck watches the sled crawl away.

The ice opens. Dogs, sled, Charles, Mercedes, and Hal vanish through the yawning hole.

Thornton kneels beside Buck, searching his bruised body with rough, gentle hands.

THORNTON
You poor devil.

Buck licks his hand.

Weeks later, SKEET, a small Irish setter, cleans Buck's healing wounds each morning. NIG, a huge black dog with laughing eyes, entices him into play.

Buck follows Thornton from tent to riverbank and watches him by the fire, fearful that this master might disappear as the others did.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Love, genuine passionate love, was his for the first time.

Thornton grips Buck's massive head between his hands and presses his forehead to Buck's.

THORNTON
God! You can all but speak!

EXT. FORTY-MILE CREEK RAPIDS - DAY

HANS and PETE, Thornton's quiet partners, guide a rope from tree to tree as Thornton poles a narrow boat through roaring rapids.

The rope checks too hard. The boat flips. Thornton is flung into the current.

Buck leaps from the bank and reaches him. Thornton grips Buck's tail, but the current drives them toward jagged rocks.

Thornton catches a rock and releases Buck.

THORNTON
Go, Buck! Go!

Buck fights to shore. Hans and Pete lash the rope around Buck's neck and shoulders and send him back into the current.

The first attempt sweeps Buck under and slams him against the bank. He coughs water, hears Thornton's voice, and rises again.

Buck launches straight into the torrent. At the exact point above Thornton, he turns downstream like a battering ram.

Thornton catches Buck around the shaggy neck. Hans snubs the rope around a tree. Dog and man vanish beneath the water, then swing hard into shore.

Thornton crawls to Buck's side as Skeet licks Buck's wet face.

THORNTON
That settles it. We camp right here.

INT. ELDORADO SALOON, DAWSON - NIGHT

Men crowd around a sled outside the open saloon door. Twenty sacks of flour, a thousand pounds in all, freeze its runners to the packed snow.

MATTHEWSON, a Bonanza King, slams a sack of gold dust on the bar.

MATTHEWSON
I've got a thousand dollars that says he can't.

THORNTON
Buck can start a thousand pounds.

JIM O'BRIEN silently places another sack beside Matthewson's, lending Thornton the stake.

Outside, Buck stands alone in his harness. His recovered body is huge and hard with muscle; his brown muzzle, pale chest blaze, and long coat shine in the bitter cold.

Thornton kneels close and rests cheek against Buck's head.

THORNTON
As you love me, Buck. As you love me.

Buck closes his teeth gently over Thornton's mittened hand.

Thornton steps back.

THORNTON
Gee!

Buck takes up the slack and jerks right. The runners crack against the ice.

THORNTON
Haw!

Buck jerks left. The runners snap free.

THORNTON
Now, mush!

Buck throws his whole body forward. His claws score the snow. The sled trembles, moves by inches, then rolls steadily toward the pile of firewood marking one hundred yards.

Buck passes the mark. The crowd erupts.

Thornton falls to his knees before Buck, cursing him softly and lovingly as he holds his head.

EXT. LOST MINE VALLEY - DUSK

The winnings finance a journey east with Thornton, Hans, Pete, Buck, and the dogs. They find a shallow placer where gold gleams like yellow butter in every pan.

Moose-hide sacks of dust pile outside their spruce-bough lodge. Thornton, Hans, and Pete work from dawn until firelight.

Buck lies beside the fire, watching the black forest beyond the gold camp.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Deep in the forest a call was sounding. As often as he heard it, mysteriously thrilling and luring, he felt compelled to turn his back upon the fire.

Buck rises and slips between the trees.

EXT. FOREST CREEK - NIGHT

Buck hears a long, strange howl. In a moonlit opening stands a long, lean timber wolf, nose lifted to the sky.

Buck approaches in a crouch. The wolf flees. Buck pursues it through creek beds and over a bleak divide until the smaller animal finally stops.

They sniff noses. They play in the wary, half-coy manner of fierce beasts, then run side by side through the forest.

At a running stream, Buck stops. The distant glow of Thornton's campfire catches in his eyes.

The wolf whines for Buck to follow. Buck turns toward the camp instead.

The wolf sits, raises its muzzle, and gives one mournful howl.

EXT. LOST MINE CAMP - DAY

After days of hunting alone, Buck returns toward camp. An unnatural silence grips the valley.

He finds Nig dead in a thicket, an arrow through his body. Farther on lies Hans, feathered with arrows beside the ruined lodge.

YEEHAT HUNTERS dance among the wreckage.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For the last time in his life, Buck allowed passion to usurp cunning and reason. It was because of his great love for John Thornton that he lost his head.

Buck roars and explodes into the clearing. He tears the throat of the foremost hunter, slashes another in passing, and spins too quickly for the arrows.

In panic, the Yeehats shoot one another and flee into the forest. Buck drives them down among the trees until the valley is empty.

He returns to find Pete dead in his blankets. At the edge of a muddy pool lies Skeet, faithful to the last.

Buck follows Thornton's scent into the water. No scent leads away.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Death, as a passing out and away from the lives of the living, he knew. John Thornton was dead.

EXT. WOLF CLEARING - NIGHT

A full moon washes the clearing beside the pool. Buck stands alone until sharp yelps rise from the dark.

A wolf pack pours into the moonlight. The boldest wolf leaps. Buck strikes once and breaks its neck.

Three more rush him. Buck pivots, snapping and gashing, then backs into the angle of a gravel bank where no wolf can come behind him.

The pack draws away, panting. The long, lean wolf from the forest creek steps forward, whining softly.

Buck and the wild brother touch noses.

An old, scarred wolf raises his muzzle to the moon and begins the long howl. One by one, the pack joins him.

Buck lifts his head and answers.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now the call came to Buck in unmistakable accents. John Thornton was dead. The last tie was broken.

Buck leaves the gravel bank. The wolves crowd around him, then turn into the trees.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the long winter nights he may be seen running at the head of the pack through pale moonlight or glimmering borealis, his great throat a-bellow as he sings the song of the younger world, which is the song of the pack.

Buck runs beside his wild brother into the forest.

> FADE OUT.

> THE END
